<article class="env-lemma" id="09GY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09GY">Lemma <span data-tag="09GY">9.11.2</span>.</a> Two polynomials in $k[x]$ are relatively prime precisely when they have no common roots in an algebraic closure $\overline{k}$ of $k$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The claim is that any two polynomials $P, Q$ generate $(1)$ in $k[x]$ if and only if they generate $(1)$ in $\overline{k}[x]$. This is a piece of linear algebra: a system of linear equations with coefficients in $k$ has a solution if and only if it has a solution in any extension of $k$. Consequently, we can reduce to the case of an algebraically closed field, in which case the result is clear from what we have already proved. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>